{"packet_id":"D041-01","doc_id":"D041","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"RSO allowable rent increase","requirement":"RSO landlords may raise rent only once every 12 months at LAHD's allowable percentage; other increases need LAHD approval. Since February 2, 2026, utility and dependent surcharges are barred; landlords may still collect a $1.61 monthly registration-fee surcharge.","key_value":"once every 12 months at the allowable rent increase percentage","coverage_conditions":"Rental properties first built on or before October 1, 1978, plus LAMC 151.28 replacement units: apartments, condominiums, townhomes, duplexes, two or more single-family units on a parcel, hotel rooms occupied over 30 days, ADUs, JADUs, mobile homes.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"built","role":"covered","op":"on_or_before","date":"1978-10-01","basis":"construction"},{"type":"other","role":"exempt","text":"Luxury exemption: owner may apply where monthly rent charged on or before May 31, 1978 qualifies"}],"per_tenancy":"Rent amount is not regulated for condominium or townhome tenancies that commenced after December 31, 1995.","coverage_quotes":["Generally, the RSO applies to rental properties that were first built on or before October 1, 1978, as well as replacement units under LAMC Section 151.28","Condominium (Rent amount is not regulated for tenancies that commenced after December 31, 1995)","A landlord can apply for an exemption from the RSO if they can establish the monthly rent charged on or before May 31, 1978,*."]},"exemptions":"Rent amount not regulated for condominium or townhome tenancies commenced after December 31, 1995; luxury exemption where the rent charged on or before May 31, 1978 qualifies; temporary exemptions.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2026-02-02","valid_through":null,"citation":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance (L.A.M.C. §151.00 et seq.)","quoted_span":"Rent may be increased once every 12 months by the allowable rent increase percentage","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D041-01","doc_id":"D041","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"RSO legal reasons for eviction","requirement":"RSO landlords may evict only for the listed at-fault or no-fault reasons; they must file termination notices with LAHD within three business days of service and pay relocation assistance for no-fault evictions.","key_value":"3 business days to file eviction notice; relocation assistance for no-fault evictions","coverage_conditions":"All rental units subject to the City's Rent Stabilization Ordinance, i.e. properties first built on or before October 1, 1978.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"built","role":"covered","op":"on_or_before","date":"1978-10-01","basis":"construction"}],"per_tenancy":"No-fault evictions (owner or family move-in, resident manager, demolition, government order, conversion to affordable housing) require relocation assistance.","coverage_quotes":["Generally, the RSO applies to rental properties that were first built on or before October 1, 1978","All notices to terminate a tenancy for all rental units subject to City’s Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO) must be filed with the Los Angeles Housing Department (LAHD) within three (3) business days of service on the tenant"]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Los Angeles Municipal Code § 151.09","quoted_span":"All notices to terminate a tenancy for all rental units subject to City’s Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO) must be filed with the Los Angeles Housing Department (LAHD) within three (3) business days of service on the tenant per Los Angeles Municipal Code 151.09.C.9 & 165.05.B.5.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D041-01","n_rules":2,"note":null}

